  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「キビ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AD%E3%83%93",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111016525,
      "text": "キビ は、イネ科の一年草で、穀物の一種。アワやヒエなどと同じくイネ科雑穀類に分類される穀類である。五穀の一つとされる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Proso millet」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proso_millet",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1368849885,
      "text": "Panicum miliaceum is a grain crop with many common names, including proso millet, broomcorn millet, common millet, hog millet, Kashfi millet, red millet, and white millet. Archaeobotanical evidence suggests millet was first domesticated about 10,000 BP in Northern China. Major cultivated areas include Northern China, Himachal Pradesh of India, Nepal, Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, the Middle East, Turkey, Romania, and the Great Plains states of the United States. About 500,000 acres are grown each year. The crop is notable both for its extremely short lifecycle, with some varieties producing grain only 60 days after planting, and its low water requirements, producing grain more efficiently per unit of moisture than any other grain species tested. The name \"proso millet\" comes from the pan-Slavic general and generic name for millet."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Proso millet」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proso_millet#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1368849885,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Proso millet is a relatively low-demanding crop, and diseases are not known; consequently, it is often used in organic farming systems in Europe. In the United States, it is often used as an intercrop. Thus, proso millet can help to avoid a summer fallow, and continuous crop rotation can be achieved. Its superficial root system and its resistance to atrazine residue make proso millet a good intercrop between two water- and pesticide-demanding crops. The stubbles of the last crop, by allowing more heat into the soil, result in a faster and earlier millet growth. While millet occupies the ground, because of its superficial root system, the soil can replenish its water content for the next crop. Later crops, for example, a winter wheat, can in turn benefit from the millet stubble, which act as snow accumulators. P. miliaceum is commonly classified into five races, miliaceum, patentissimum, contractum, compactum, and ovatum.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
